  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 140 PM CDT Sat Oct 31 2026\n\nCurrent surface analysis places low stretching from eastern\nWyoming into eastern Colorado, with high over the eastern Great\nLakes. Upper level analysis places low off the west coast, with\ntilted ridge stretching from the Ohio River Valley into the \nUpper Midwest. Over our area, low level moisture continues to \nlift in ahead and around the aforementioned surface low, \nresulting in low clouds along with some fog over our area. Some \nof the fog continues to be dense over far southwest North Dakota\nwhere a Dense Fog Advisory remains in places. \n\nFor tonight, low clouds will remain in place along with areas of\nfog. With not much relief in visibility expected over the far\nsouthwest, have decided to extend the Dense Fog Advisory through\nthe overnight hours into early Sunday morning. Occasional\nperiods of drizzle and/or light rain will be possible as a\nvariety of weak waves lift through the upper flow ahead of the\naforementioned Pacific system which will move well onshore\novernight. Temperatures will remain quite mild overnight for\nthis time of year. \n\nOn Sunday, clouds will remain over the area given the\naforementioned moisture in place, with temperatures continuing \nto be rather mild. A few waves passing through will continue to \nbring occasional precipitation chances through the day, but this\nwill be on a notable increase from southwest to northeast \novernight. Aforementioned Pacific system will push over the \nRockies overnight, with a surface low deepening over Colorado \nbefore ejecting into the central plains. \n\nAs we get into Monday, high precipitation chances will continue\nover the area as surface low lifts from the central plains into\nthe upper Midwest. Widespread rains over the area are expected,\nwith the greatest rainfall expected over southeastern portions\nof the area. Latest NBM guidance now indicates a 60 to 90\npercent of seeing 1 inch of rain or greater along and southeast\nof a line from Linton to Jamestown, with a 50 to 70 percent\nchance of seeing 2 inches or more over the southern James River\nValley which includes Edgeley, LaMoure, Ellendale, and Oakes. In\naddition, with the surface low shifting northeast, winds will be\non the increase over the area, especially along the South Dakota\nborder where latest guidance indicates north-northwesterly winds\nwith gusts to 45 mph possible. \n\nThereafter some questions remain with the forecast as ensemble guidance\nhas been leaning towards ridging, but some deterministic\nguidancecontinues to lean towards a bit more active pattern \nwith some modest short wave troughs moving through. NBM \ntemperatures spreads continue to indicate near to above average \ntemperatures.",
